title: The Biblical Truth About Perseverance of the Saints
description: How do you know you're saved? The false doctrine of "perseverance" set beside Once Saved, Always Saved and the objective, finished work of Christ.
output: lessons/heresies/perseverance-of-the-saints.html

# From studies/data/notes/perseverance-of-the-saints-notes.html, in the author's words, arranged into slides.
# Verse text is pulled from the KJV by the builder. Short verse fragments quoted inside the notes' prose
# (Matthew 24:21-22, James 2:18) are shown as the full verses instead. "John 15" links to the chapter in the app.

=== Introduction
--- The Biblical Truth About Perseverance of the Saints vs Eternal Security [title]
overline: Answering error
heading: The Biblical Truth About Perseverance of the Saints
sub: vs Eternal Security
big: 1 John 5:13 | mark: that ye may know that ye have eternal life
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the slides. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- How do you know you're saved?
p: When asked "How do you know you're saved?", many religious systems answer with uncertainty:
- "If I persevere to the end..."
- "If I continue in faith and good works..."
- "If I keep the sacraments and don't fall away..."
callout-honest: Their assurance rests on what they have done or will do—always looking inward at performance.

--- He did it all
p: But the Bible-believing Christian answers differently:
- "Because Jesus died for my sins and rose again—He did it all!"
- "Because God, who cannot lie, promised everlasting life the moment I believed."
big: Titus 1:2
big: John 5:24

--- Ye may know
lead: Today we'll expose the false doctrine of "perseverance" taught in many theologies, contrast it with biblical Once Saved, Always Saved in context, and rejoice in the objective, finished work of Christ that ye MAY KNOW we have eternal life by faith alone.

=== I. Other theologies
--- What Other Theologies Teach About "Perseverance of the Saints"
panel: Calvinism/Reformed Theology | True saints will persevere in holiness and good works as inevitable evidence of election. If you don't endure in faithfulness, you were never truly saved. (Tied to their "P" in TULIP)
panel: Arminianism/Wesleyan Theology | Believers can lose salvation if they don't persevere in faith and obedience; "enduring to the end" is required to be finally saved.

--- Rome and Luther
panel: Roman Catholicism | Salvation is maintained through sacraments, cooperation with grace, and avoiding mortal sin—no full assurance until death. In fact, Catholicism officially teaches that claiming to know with 100% certainty that you are in a state of grace (and will persevere to the end) is the sin of presumption against the Holy Spirit.
panel: Lutheranism (varying views) | Some emphasize perseverance through Word and Sacrament; assurance is hoped for but often tied to ongoing faith/repentance.

--- Common thread
callout: **Common thread:** Assurance is subjective—based on personal endurance, fruit, or continued performance. No one can fully know until the end.

=== II. Enduring to the end
--- What "Perseverance" Actually Means in Context
lead: These phrases are not about eternal salvation but physical deliverance/safety through tribulation and persecution for kingdom inheritance and rewards.
big: Matthew 24:13

--- The Olivet Discourse
p: Context: Olivet Discourse about the Great Tribulation ([[Matthew 24:21|v. 21]]). "Saved" here means delivered physically/safely from tribulation, the persecution of believers in this world (as in [[Matthew 24:22|v. 22]]).
scripture: Matthew 24:21-22 | mark: For then shall be great tribulation; there should no flesh be saved
p: **Revelation 2–3 (letters to churches, not individual people!):** "Overcometh" and hold fast for rewards, crowns, and ruling with Christ—not gaining or losing salvation.

--- Biblical perseverance
lead: Biblical perseverance for believers today: Standing firm in faith amid trials, but eternal security is guaranteed by God's power, not ours.
big: James 1:12
big: 1 Peter 5:9

--- Guaranteed by God's power
big: John 10:28-29
big: Philippians 1:6

=== III. "Repent of your sins"
--- Probation, not justification
lead: The modern demand to "repent of your sins" (turn from all sin to be saved or stay saved) confuses biblical repentance (a change of mind) with works—it turns the gospel into probation (ongoing performance required) instead of instant justification by faith alone.
big: Romans 4:5
big: Ephesians 2:8-9

--- God Himself "repents"
p: God Himself "repents" multiple times in Scripture (over a dozen), yet He cannot sin—proving repentance means a change of mind or course, not turning from sin.
scripture: Jonah 3:9-10 | mark: God repented of the evil
scripture: Genesis 6:6; Exodus 32:14

--- Sinners repent about Christ
lead: Sinners repent (change mind) about Christ—believing the gospel—not promising to clean up their life first or maintain it afterward.

=== IV. Hypocrisy and spiritual pride
--- The leaven of the Pharisees
p: It breeds fruit inspectors and holier-than-thou attitudes—the leaven of the Pharisees, which Jesus directly calls hypocrisy.
scripture: Matthew 16:6; Matthew 16:12
note: (The leaven is hypocrisy and false doctrine—[[Luke 12:1]])

--- I am not as other men are
big: Luke 18:11-12
p: It's hypocritical back-door works salvation:
- Claims "grace alone" but ties assurance to ongoing effort.
- Makes perseverance the root instead of the fruit—turning the gospel upside down.

--- Good works aren't automatic
p: Good works aren't automatic—if you don't walk in the Spirit, don't expect to do the works God has appointed for you ([[Ephesians 2:10|Eph. 2:10]]).
- True fruit is primarily the others we bring to Christ with the gospel ([[Romans 1:13|Rom. 1:13]]; [[John 4:36]]).
- Works will be rewarded at the Judgment Seat, but even if they are wood, hay, stubble—you yourself are still saved.
big: 1 Corinthians 3:15

--- But not before God
lead: Men may justify us by outward works, but not before God.
big: James 2:24
note: (Context: justified before men, [[James 2:18|v. 18]]—"shew me thy faith")
scripture: Romans 4:1-2 | mark: but not before God

=== V. Doubt
--- How It Creates Doubt
- For the seeking sinner: "I must turn from sins, make Jesus Lord, and persevere" becomes vague, hopeless, and impossible—leading to trust in self instead of Christ.
- Lordship Salvation / "repent of your sins" / "make Jesus Lord of your life" is subjective and undefined—what sin level disqualifies? How much commitment is enough?
- Keeps people from simple trust: They never rest because they're always inspecting fruit or fearing failure.

--- Robs Believers of Joy
lead: For the believer: Constant doubt—"Have I endured enough? Is my faith real?"—steals peace and assurance God intended.
big: Romans 5:1
big: Colossians 2:2

=== VI. Objective assurance
--- A present possession
lead: Salvation is received the moment you believe—eternal life is a present possession.
big: John 5:24
big: John 6:47

--- Sealed
lead: Assurance is objective: Anchored in God's unbreakable promises and Christ's resurrection, not our performance.
scripture: Ephesians 1:13-14 | mark: ye were sealed with that holy Spirit of promise
big: Ephesians 4:30

--- Kept by the power of God
big: Hebrews 7:25
big: 1 Peter 1:5
big: Jude 1:24

--- Commanded to know
lead: We are commanded to know we have eternal life—not hope or examine fruit.
big: 1 John 5:13 | mark: that ye may know that ye have eternal life
p: Fruit follows naturally from abiding in Christ ([[app:John-15|John 15]]), but it's never the basis of assurance.

=== VII. Conclusion
--- Rest in the Sure Promises of God
- Reject vague, hopeless demands ("make Jesus Lord," "repent of sins," "persevere in works").
- Trust the simple, objective gospel: Christ died for your sins, rose again, and gives eternal life freely to whosoever believeth.
scripture: 1 Corinthians 15:3-4

--- Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ
big: Acts 16:31
big: Romans 8:38-39
callout: How do you know you're saved? Because God said it, and He cannot lie.

--- Jesus' Unbreakable Promise of Eternal Security
scripture: John 6:37-40; John 6:44; John 6:47 | mark: him that cometh to me I will in no wise cast out; I will raise him up at the last day

--- In no wise cast out
point: Raised up at the last day | Jesus promises four times in this chapter that He will raise up every believer at the last day—it's the Father's will, and Christ guarantees He will lose nothing.
point: Never rejected | "In no wise cast out" means once you come to Him by faith, you're never rejected or lost—eternal security from the Lord's own mouth.
point: Do it today | If you've never trusted Christ alone—do it today. Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and thou shalt be saved.

--- Believer—stop doubting [title]
heading: Believer—stop doubting.
lead: Don’t let the hypocrites and the holier-than-thous make you doubt your salvation. Rejoice in your eternal security and live for the One who saved you completely!
